== 6.1 What Are Noncommunicable Diseases?

*Noncommunicable diseases* (NCDs) are generally not passed directly from person to person as infections are. Major examples include cardiovascular diseases, cancers, diabetes and chronic respiratory diseases. They often involve a combination of genetic, physiological, environmental and behavioural factors. Some infections can increase the risk of particular cancers, so describing a condition as an NCD does not mean infections and NCDs can never be related.

Cardiovascular diseases affect the heart and blood vessels. Diabetes involves problems regulating blood glucose; the causes and management differ by type and person. Cancers involve abnormal cell growth and include many different diseases. Chronic respiratory diseases affect breathing and lung function. These brief definitions are not diagnostic tests.

#rule-box("Category is not a diagnosis")[
  Identifying an example of an NCD in an exam does not determine whether a particular person has it. Screening, diagnosis and treatment require appropriate professional care.
]

== 6.2 Risk and Prevention

Some risk factors are modifiable; others, such as age or inherited characteristics, are not readily changed. Tobacco exposure, harmful alcohol use, an unhealthy dietary pattern, physical inactivity and air pollution are among factors associated with NCD risk. A risk factor increases probability; it does not mean that everyone exposed will develop disease or that a person without that factor cannot be affected.

Risk reduction may include avoiding tobacco smoke, appropriate physical activity, a varied diet and access to preventive healthcare. These actions are influenced by income, facilities, safe spaces, policies and other conditions. Screening or treatment may be appropriate depending on age, risk and local guidance. Do not stop prescribed medication because a general study book recommends healthy habits.

