== 6.3 Mental Wellbeing and Support

Mental health is part of health. People experience stress, sadness and anxiety differently; a difficult day does not by itself establish a mental health diagnosis. Persistent distress, difficulties with daily life or safety concerns can warrant professional assessment. A mental health condition is not a sign of weakness or a moral failing.

Support can include listening without judgement, involving a trusted person, and helping someone access qualified care. Sleep, social connection, activity and practical support can contribute to wellbeing, but they do not replace treatment when treatment is needed. Avoid promising confidentiality when someone's immediate safety is at risk; involve appropriate emergency or safeguarding services according to local procedures.

#concept-box("Support without diagnosing")[
  Listen, take the concern seriously and help the person find suitable support. Do not diagnose from a single behaviour or promise that one activity will cure every condition.
]

== 6.4 Urgent Symptoms and Care

Some symptoms need urgent assessment regardless of the suspected cause. Sudden severe chest pain, fainting, severe breathing difficulty or sudden weakness on one side of the body can be emergencies. Call local emergency services and follow dispatcher instructions; do not delay because a textbook cannot establish the diagnosis. If someone is at immediate risk of harming themselves, seek urgent local emergency or crisis support and do not leave them alone when it is safe to stay.

Management of long-term conditions is individual. A qualified professional can discuss testing, medicines, follow-up and lifestyle support. Respect privacy and avoid assumptions about a person's condition or abilities.

== 6.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "An NCD example", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [Which is an example of a noncommunicable disease? \
A. Diabetes \
B. Malaria \
C. Tuberculosis \
D. Measles],
  [Diabetes is an NCD. Malaria, TB and measles are infections with different transmission routes. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "Risk is not certainty", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A factor raises the probability of a condition. What follows? \
A. Every exposed person will develop it \
B. The risk may be higher, but the outcome is not certain \
C. Other influences cannot matter \
D. A diagnosis has already been made],
  [A risk factor affects probability, not certainty or a complete diagnosis. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "Supporting a friend", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A friend reports persistent distress and difficulty managing daily life. The most helpful general response is to \
A. ridicule the concern \
B. diagnose a condition immediately \
C. listen respectfully and help them seek suitable support \
D. promise that one activity will cure every problem],
  [Listen without judgement and help the person access appropriate support. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "Urgent symptoms", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [A person develops sudden one-sided weakness and difficulty speaking. What should a bystander do? \
A. Wait several days for a textbook diagnosis \
B. Give another person's prescription \
C. Assume it is harmless because symptoms started suddenly \
D. Seek emergency help immediately and follow dispatcher instructions],
  [Sudden neurological symptoms can indicate a medical emergency. Seek urgent help rather than delaying for a diagnosis. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("Risk and blame")[
  A risk factor does not identify the cause of every case. A person can develop an NCD without a particular risk factor, and environmental or social conditions can affect prevention options.
]

